 limit does not exist?","options":["$f$ is not differentiable at $x_0$; $f'(x_0)$ undefined","$f'(x_0)$ is set equal to $0$.","$f$ is automatically continuous everywhere","$x_0$ is removed from $X$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a26585a7c49b5aba8b7fa89e","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_derivative_and_differentiability_e5b63888_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which function is differentiable everywhere?","options":["$f(x) = |x|$ at $x = 0$","The Dirichlet function","A function not defined near $x_0$","$f(x) = x^2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e00d78a2929bf29c361c6e3b","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_derivative_and_differentiability_e5b63888_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Is $f(x)=|x|$ differentiable at $x=0$?","options":["It is not differentiable at $x = 0$.","It is differentiable at $x = 0$ with derivative $1$.","It is not defined at $x = 0$.","It has derivative $0$ at every point."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ef1ee29e5bc6c4cf48ab38e1","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_derivative_and_differentiability_e5b63888_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the correct connection between differentiability and continuity?","options":["Continuity always implies differentiability.","Differentiability implies that the derivative is zero.","Differentiability implies continuity.","Continuity makes the derivative non-unique."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"76c0e84fa9e073b5365363f4","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_derivative_and_differentiability_e5b63888_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does uniqueness mean for the derivative?","options":["A function can have many different derivative values at the same point.","If the derivative exists at a point, its value is unique.","The derivative exists only for one point of the domain.","Every tangent line must be vertical."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4080c52446be07994e980475","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_derivative_and_differentiability_e5b63888_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Which function is not differentiable at $x=\\tfrac12$?","options":["$f(x)=\\dfrac1{(x+2)^2}$","$f(x)=\\sin\\left(x-\\tfrac12\\right)$","$f(x)=|4x^2-1|$","$f(x)=|2x+1|$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"064e7939abdbc152c2452e72","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_derivative_and_differentiability_e5b63888_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Does continuity of $f$ at $x_0$ imply that $f$ is differentiable there?","options":["No; continuity alone does not imply differentiability","Yes; continuity always implies differentiability","Yes; but only for functions that are polynomials","Yes; but only when the centre value is zero"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"bed1587d4e132b3741dd8197","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_derivative_and_differentiability_e5b63888_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which extra domain condition is required for differentiability at $x_0$?","options":["$f$ is defined on some interval around $x_0$","$f$ is defined only at $x_0$.","$x_0$ must be the largest point of $X$","$f$ must be positive near $x_0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e96235c55511ad3ebde2c47f","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_derivative_and_differentiability_e5b63888_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If a real-valued function $f$ is differentiable at an interior point $x_0$, how can its graph be approximated near $x_0$?","options":["The curve can be approximated locally by a linear function.","The function must be increasing near the point.","The point must be outside the domain.","The graph must have a vertical tangent."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d52e5c50b88fcf09fff3028d","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_derivative_and_differentiability_e5b63888_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x) = |x|$ at $x_0 = 0$, the difference quotient equals $1$ for $x \\gt 0$ and $-1$ for $x \\lt 0$. What follows?","options":["The derivative at $0$ equals $1$.","The derivative at $0$ equals $-1$.","The derivative at $0$ equals $0$.","The derivative at $0$ does not exist."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2c476b3df9d91698a3b6a601","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_derivative_and_differentiability_e5b63888_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"A function is defined only at the single point $x_0$ and nowhere in any neighbourhood around $x_0$. What should we say about differentiability at $x_0$?","options":["It is differentiable with derivative $0$.","It is differentiable with derivative $1$.","It is not differentiable at $x_0$.","It is automatically differentiable because $f(x_0)$ exists."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"264df8d8110d377fbcabc982","time_limit":40},{"id":"expansion_pack_014_derivative_and_differentiability_new_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does the derivative measure at a point?","options":["The number of points in the domain.","The length of the graph.","The value of the independent variable only.","Instantaneous rate of change."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ed16a34421e124253c707028","time_limit":20},{"id":"expansion_pack_014_derivative_and_differentiability_new_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Using the definition, what is $f'(x_0)$ for $f(x)=x^2$?","options":["$2x_0$","$x_0^2$","$x_0$","$2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a108bbb1df9727a2b342e3ff","time_limit":30},{"id":"expansion_pack_014_derivative_and_differentiability_new_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which tangent-line description matches differentiability?","options":["There is a non-vertical tangent line at the point.","There must be a vertical tangent line at the point.","No tangent-line interpretation is possible.","The tangent line must be horizontal."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"40293283749a3f571859a687","time_limit":20},{"id":"expansion_pack_014_derivative_and_differentiability_new_004","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Define $f(x)=x\\sin(1/x)$ for $x\\neq0$ and $f(0)=0$. Which statement about $f$ at $0$ is correct?","options":["$f$ is differentiable at $0$ with $f'(0)=0$, because $f(x)\\to0$.","$f$ is continuous at $0$ but not differentiable at $0$.","$\\lim_{x\\to0}f(x)$ does not exist, because $\\sin(1/x)$ oscillates.","$f$ is not continuous at $0$, because $x\\sin(1/x)$ is undefined at $0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a1c1293fe12afd934ab88e9d","time_limit":45},{"id":"expansion_pack_014_derivative_and_differentiability_new_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If $f$ is discontinuous at $x_0$, what follows about differentiability there?","options":["$f$ is not differentiable at $x_0$","$f$ is still differentiable at $x_0$","$f'(x_0)=0$","Nothing can be said either way"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d63a9ba983fd95f3cbad41f0","time_limit":30},{"id":"expansion_pack_014_derivative_and_differentiability_new_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If $f'(x_0)=0$, what does this say about the tangent line at $x_0$?","options":["The tangent line is horizontal","The tangent line is vertical","$f$ is undefined at $x_0$","$f$ must have a corner at $x_0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ea1973750b3cbb7ff78bc1ad","time_limit":25},{"id":"expansion_pack_014_derivative_and_differentiability_new_007","difficulty":"standard","ques